# software-factory: the local schedule, the crontab equivalent of the
# standing-agents and build workflows. Every line is commented out: copy the ones you
# want into `crontab -e`, with /path/to/project replaced by a clone of the project.
#
# - Use a clone that does nothing else and stays on the base branch (main below; use
#   yours). run-agent.sh judges with the scripts and factory.config.yaml of the checkout
#   it runs from, so each line fast-forwards it first and starts nothing if that fails:
#   a stale checkout, or one on another branch, would run old or unapproved rules.
# - Local runs use your own gh login (`gh auth status`) and your git credentials, not a
#   CI secret. If they can push workflow files (the workflow scope), the "agents can't
#   change .github/workflows" barrier does not hold for these runs; for unattended runs,
#   use a fine-grained token without the Workflows permission.
# - FACTORY_HARNESS_CMD, when set, overrides harness_cmd from factory.config.yaml for
#   these runs (for example a harness command with guarded local permissions instead of
#   the CI bypass flags).
# - The stop switch still applies: nothing starts unless the repo variable
#   FACTORY_ENABLED is "true". Leave FACTORY_CI_AGENTS off while this schedule is in
#   use, so CI agents never race these runs (run-agent.sh's role locks stop two runs of
#   one standing role either way).
# - Logs are appended under .factory/ (gitignored, never state). Each run also writes
#   .factory/logs/<agent id>.log.
# - cron starts with a minimal PATH: give it the directories that hold bash, git, gh,
#   jq and the harness binary.
#
# SHELL=/bin/bash
# PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
#
# Standing roles (bug-fix, perf, babysit, ship): each one that is due, at minute 17 of
# every hour (roles.<role>.every decides what is due).
# 17 * * * * cd /path/to/project && mkdir -p .factory && { git pull -q --ff-only origin main && bash scripts/run-agent.sh --due; } >>.factory/cron-due.log 2>&1
#
# The build chain (intake, plan-features or the next ready feature), every 30 minutes.
# Two build runs can overlap: each claims a different ready feature, or finds nothing.
# */30 * * * * cd /path/to/project && mkdir -p .factory && { git pull -q --ff-only origin main && bash scripts/run-agent.sh --role build; } >>.factory/cron-build.log 2>&1
#
# The same, with another harness command for local runs only:
# 17 * * * * cd /path/to/project && mkdir -p .factory && { git pull -q --ff-only origin main && FACTORY_HARNESS_CMD='my-harness --flags' bash scripts/run-agent.sh --due; } >>.factory/cron-due.log 2>&1
#
# Windows: Task Scheduler can run the same commands through Git Bash, e.g.
#   "C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe" -lc "cd /c/path/to/project && mkdir -p .factory && { git pull -q --ff-only origin main && bash scripts/run-agent.sh --due; } >>.factory/cron-due.log 2>&1"
